---
scope: src/tools/upgrade
intent: >
  aide_upgrade is the explicit "bring this host project up to date with
  canonical" tool. It exists because aide_init is idempotent and never
  overwrites existing files — which protects customizations but means
  host projects get stale when canonical docs, commands, or the pointer
  stub are updated upstream. aide_upgrade closes that gap: it replaces
  every driftable methodology artifact on the host's disk with the
  current canonical version, reports what changed, and warns before it
  does so. A two-phase dry-run-then-confirm flow gives the caller a
  chance to review the damage before any file is touched.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Without `confirm: true`, the tool returns a dry-run preview
      listing every upgradable file with a prospective status —
      `would update` (content differs from canonical), `unchanged`
      (already matches canonical), or `would create` (missing on
      host) — plus an explicit warning that customizations to
      commands and docs will be overwritten on confirm. No file on
      disk is modified during dry-run.
    - With `confirm: true`, every methodology artifact is overwritten
      with the current canonical version in a single pass — every
      pipeline slash command (the orchestrator entry point and all
      phase commands), every methodology doc in the `.aide/docs/`
      hub, the hub index, the pointer stub's marker-bounded region
      in the host's config file, the MCP server registration, and
      IDE configuration. The upgrade surface is all-or-nothing;
      there is no per-file opt-out.
    - After upgrading, each file is reported with a final status:
      `updated` (content differed and was replaced), `unchanged`
      (already matched canonical), or `created` (was missing and
      has been written, same as init would do). The caller reads
      the summary once and knows exactly what changed on disk.
    - Files outside the methodology surface are never touched. User
      code, user `.aide` specs, and any file not installed by
      aide_init are invisible to this tool.
    - The dry-run warning is unambiguous: it names the specific
      categories of files that will be overwritten and states that
      any local customizations in those files will be lost. The
      agent calling the tool is expected to present this warning to
      the user before confirming.
  undesired:
    - An upgrade that silently overwrites files without a prior
      dry-run preview. The two-phase flow exists because the tool
      destructively replaces content the user may have customized;
      skipping the preview turns a controlled upgrade into a
      surprise data loss.
    - An upgrade that touches user code, user `.aide` specs, or
      any file outside the methodology surface. The upgrade scope
      is the set of files aide_init installed — nothing else.
    - A dry-run that modifies files on disk. The preview phase is
      read-only; any side effect during dry-run is a bug, not an
      optimization.
    - An upgrade that offers per-file granularity — "update this
      doc but keep that command." Partial upgrades create a host
      whose methodology artifacts are at mixed versions, which is
      harder to reason about than either fully-stale or
      fully-current. All-or-nothing is the deliberate constraint.
    - A dry-run whose warning is vague ("some files may change")
      or absent. The warning must name the artifact categories and
      state that customizations will be lost, because the caller
      needs that information to make an informed confirm decision.
    - An upgrade that reports only a success/failure boolean instead
      of per-file statuses. The caller needs to know which files
      actually changed, which were already current, and which were
      newly created — the same reporting shape aide_init provides.
---

## Context

aide_init is deliberately conservative: if a file exists, it stays. That
invariant protects every customization the user has made to slash commands,
methodology docs, even the pointer stub. But it also means a host project
initialized six months ago still carries the six-month-old versions of
every artifact init installed. When a canonical doc is rewritten, a command
template is expanded, or the pointer stub learns a new teaching sentence,
init has no path to deliver the update — re-running it just reports
"already exists" for every step. The host drifts silently, and the drift
compounds with every upstream improvement.

aide_upgrade is the controlled demolition that closes this gap. It
deliberately does what init deliberately refuses to do: overwrite every
methodology artifact with the current canonical version. The two tools
form a complementary pair — init is safe to run without thinking, upgrade
is safe to run after thinking. The two-phase dry-run-then-confirm flow is
the mechanism that converts "after thinking" from a social contract into a
tool-enforced one: the caller always sees the blast radius before any file
is touched, and must explicitly opt in to the overwrite.

The upgrade surface is exactly the set of files init installs — pipeline
slash commands, methodology docs, the hub index, the pointer stub, MCP
wiring, IDE configuration. User code and user `.aide` specs are outside
the surface and invisible to the tool. This boundary is load-bearing:
upgrade must feel safe to confirm precisely because its scope is narrow
and predictable.

## Strategy

**Upgrade reuses init's helpers but bypasses their skip-if-exists guard.**
The init helpers — `writeMethodology`, `installMethodologyDocs`,
`scaffoldCommands`, `wireMcp`, `configureIde` — already know how to read
canonical content, resolve host paths, and write files. Each helper's
idempotency check is what prevents init from overwriting. Upgrade calls
the same content-reading and path-resolution logic but replaces the
existence check with a content comparison: read the canonical version,
read the host version, compare, and write if they differ. The helpers are
the single source of truth for *which* files belong to the methodology
surface; upgrade should never maintain a parallel file list.

**Dry-run and confirm share a single code path with a write gate.** Both
phases walk the same file list, read the same canonical content, and
perform the same byte comparison against the host. The only difference is
what happens after the comparison: dry-run records a prospective status
(`would update`, `unchanged`, `would create`) and moves on; confirm
records a final status (`updated`, `unchanged`, `created`) and writes the
file. A boolean flag controls the gate. This eliminates the class of bugs
where dry-run and confirm disagree about what would change, because they
literally run the same logic.

**Status is determined by byte comparison, not by file presence alone.**
Init can get away with a simple existence check because it never
overwrites. Upgrade cannot — it needs to distinguish "file exists and
matches canonical" from "file exists and differs." The comparison is
byte-level: read the canonical content from `readCanonicalDoc`, read the
host file from disk, compare. If the host file is missing, the status is
`would create` / `created`. If the bytes match, the status is `unchanged`.
If the bytes differ, the status is `would update` / `updated`. No diffing
algorithm is needed; the tool reports *that* files changed, not *how* they
changed — the caller can run their own diff tool after the fact.

**The pointer stub is replaced within its marker boundaries.** The stub
lives inside a larger config file (CLAUDE.md, cursor rules, etc.) that
contains user content outside the markers. Upgrade must replace only the
marker-bounded region, leaving everything before the opening marker and
after the closing marker untouched. This is the one write step that
cannot simply overwrite the whole file — it must parse, splice, and
rewrite. The markers are already byte-stable across refactors (init's
`getMethodologyMarker` guarantees this), so the splice is reliable.

**MCP wiring and IDE configuration follow the same overwrite-if-differs
pattern.** The MCP config entry for the `aide` server is compared against
the canonical shape and replaced if it differs; other servers in the
config are untouched. Zed and VS Code configuration are re-applied
unconditionally since they are additive (a Markdown file-type association,
an extension install) and already idempotent in their own right.

**The dry-run warning is composed, not a static string.** The warning
enumerates the artifact categories that contain at least one `would update`
or `would create` entry — "slash commands", "methodology docs", "pointer
stub" — and states that local customizations in those categories will be
lost. If every file is `unchanged`, the warning is replaced with a
confirmation that the host is already current. This keeps the warning
honest: it names only the categories that will actually be affected, so
the caller is not alarmed by a generic message when only one doc has
drifted.

**All-or-nothing is enforced at the tool boundary, not at the helper
level.** The confirm flag gates the entire upgrade — there is no
per-helper or per-file opt-out. This is a deliberate constraint: a host
whose methodology artifacts are at mixed canonical versions is harder to
reason about than one that is either fully stale or fully current. The
tool trades granularity for predictability.

## Good examples

A developer runs `aide_upgrade` without `confirm` on a host project that
has drifted behind canonical:

> AIDE upgrade preview (claude framework):
>
>   ~ Methodology pointer: would update
>   = .aide/docs/aide-spec.md: unchanged
>   ~ .aide/docs/aide-template.md: would update
>   = .aide/docs/plan-aide.md: unchanged
>   = .aide/docs/todo-aide.md: unchanged
>   = .aide/docs/progressive-disclosure.md: unchanged
>   = .aide/docs/agent-readable-code.md: unchanged
>   ~ .aide/docs/automated-qa.md: would update
>   = .aide/docs/index.md: unchanged
>   = aide: unchanged
>   ~ aide:research: would update
>   = aide:spec: unchanged
>   = aide:synthesize: unchanged
>   = aide:plan: unchanged
>   = aide:build: unchanged
>   = aide:qa: unchanged
>   = aide:fix: unchanged
>   = MCP config: unchanged
>   = Zed config: unchanged
>   = VS Code extension: unchanged
>
>   Warning: confirming will overwrite local customizations in:
>   methodology docs, slash commands, pointer stub.
>
>   To proceed, call aide_upgrade with confirm: true.

The developer reviews the list, decides they are comfortable losing their
local edits to `research.md` and the two methodology docs, and re-runs
with `confirm: true`. The summary now reads `updated` where it previously
read `would update`, and `unchanged` where it previously read `unchanged`.

A developer runs `aide_upgrade` on a host project that is already fully
current:

> AIDE upgrade preview (claude framework):
>
>   All 20 methodology artifacts match canonical. Nothing to upgrade.

No warning, no confirm prompt, no files touched. The tool recognized that
the host is current and said so plainly.

A developer runs `aide_upgrade` with `confirm: true` on a host that was
initialized but is missing a newly-added command (a new pipeline phase was
added upstream after their last init):

> AIDE upgraded (claude framework):
>
>   = Methodology pointer: unchanged
>   ...
>   + aide:newphase: created
>   ...
>
>   1 file created, 0 files updated, 19 files unchanged.

The missing command was created with the same status label init would use.
The upgrade surface expanded to include the new canonical artifact without
the developer needing to re-run init.

## Bad examples

An upgrade that overwrites files the moment the tool is called, with no
prior dry-run. The developer customized three slash commands over the
past month; they are gone, and the only recovery is `git checkout`. The
two-phase flow exists precisely to prevent this — skipping it turns a
controlled operation into a destructive surprise.

An upgrade that offers `--only=docs` or `--only=commands` flags so the
developer can upgrade methodology docs without touching slash commands.
The host now has docs at version N and commands at version N-2; an agent
reading the spec references a concept the command template does not yet
include; the mismatch surfaces as a confusing runtime failure three
sessions later. All-or-nothing prevents mixed-version hosts.

A dry-run that reports every file as `would update` because it compared
file paths instead of file contents. The developer sees twenty files
flagged for replacement, assumes a major upstream change, confirms, and
finds that only two files actually differed. The comparison was presence-
based, not content-based, and the inflated preview eroded trust in the
tool's reporting.

An upgrade that replaces the entire config file when updating the pointer
stub, wiping out everything the user had written above and below the
marker-bounded region. The stub replacement must splice within the
markers; anything outside them belongs to the user.

A dry-run whose warning reads "some files may be overwritten" without
naming which categories are affected. The developer has no way to assess
the blast radius without manually diffing each file. The warning failed
its only job — giving the caller enough information to make an informed
confirm decision.
